<!--
    calltargettype (GH-869): an indirect call through a target the option
    cannot retype (a parameter, a global, a record field) is cast to the
    call's own type at the call.

    Stage: P5 type recovery, just before casts are decided, and the P9 cast
    decision for the target of a CALLIND. P0 assertion: option calltargettype
    on|off.

    Source, gcc 11 -O1 -fno-pic -fcf-protection=none, linked with text at
    0x100000 and gfp at 0x200000:

        struct ops { int tag; int (*add)(int, long); void (*done)(void); };
        int (*gfp)(int, long);
        int viaparam(int (*f)(int, long), int x) { return f(x, 3) + 1; }
        int viaglobal(int x) { return gfp(x, 5) * 2; }
        int viafield(struct ops *o, int x) { o->done(); return o->add(x, o->tag); }

    gfp is mapped void *, and the record's two callbacks are declared void *
    and code *, the way a DWARF function-pointer type reaches kuna.

    Pass 1 (ctypes on, option calltargettype off, the shipped catalog default)
    is the bug: each target is a pointer C cannot call, so every call is a call
    of a void * (asserts 1 to 4), which no C compiler accepts. Pass 2 (ctypes
    on, calltargettype on, what the aggressive preset selects) leaves the
    parameter, the global and the fields their types, since other functions see
    them, and casts each target to the function-pointer type its call states
    (asserts 5 to 8). The local the void * field is copied into takes that type
    itself, as it did before, and is called without a cast (asserts 9, 10).
    Pass 3 (Rust output, calltargettype on) spells every code pointer
    *const () whatever its prototype, so it adds no cast (asserts 11, 12).

    Assertions span the combined output of the three passes.
-->
